+### stream_mic
+
+<table>
+    <tr>
+        <td>Description</td>
+        <td colspan="2">
+            Whether to accept microphone audio from clients that support it. The host mixes every
+            connected client's microphone into a single virtual microphone that host applications
+            record from, so a voice-chat application running under the stream sees the client's
+            microphone as a normal input device.
+            @note{On FreeBSD/Linux the virtual microphone is a PipeWire node named
+            @code{}sunshine-mic@endcode{}. It is created on demand when the first client completes
+            microphone setup, and requires a running PipeWire daemon. Applications select it as
+            @code{}sunshine-mic.monitor@endcode{} in the device list, or as the monitor device of
+            @code{}Sunshine Microphone@endcode{}.}
+        </td>
+    </tr>
+    <tr>
+        <td>Default</td>
+        <td colspan="2">@code{}
+            enabled
+            @endcode</td>
+    </tr>
+    <tr>
+        <td>Example</td>
+        <td colspan="2">@code{}
+            stream_mic = disabled
+            @endcode</td>
+    </tr>
+</table>
